\section{Scaling Decision Strategy}

DIFS\_OC\_v2 employs a dual-threshold decision strategy that separates container-level scaling from model-level switching.

\subsection{$\rho$-Based Horizontal Scaling}

The autoscaler monitors $\rho$, the worker utilization ratio: $\rho = \frac{\text{arrival rate}}{\text{service rate} \times \text{worker count}}$. Two thresholds govern container-level decisions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Scale-up ($\rho > 0.80$):} System near saturation. Adds a new worker.
  \item \textbf{Scale-down ($\rho < 0.30$):} System underutilized. Removes an idle worker after a cooldown.
\end{itemize}

The hysteresis band ($0.30$--$0.80$) prevents oscillatory behaviour.

\subsection{INFaaS Cost-Model (Strategies A vs B)}

For model-switching decisions, the autoscaler compares two strategies:

\textbf{Strategy A (sum of per-variant costs):}
\[
\text{cost}_{\text{A}} = \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}} \left( \text{load\_time}_m \times \text{worker\_count}_m + \text{load\_time}_m / \max(\text{QPS}_m, 0.01) \right)
\]

\textbf{Strategy B (fastest variant only):}
\[
\text{cost}_{\text{B}} = \min_{m \in \mathcal{M}} \text{load\_time}_m \times \text{total\_workers}
\]

If $\text{cost}_{\text{A}} > 1.5 \times \text{cost}_{\text{B}}$, the system switches to the fastest variant on the least-busy worker; otherwise it retains the current model mix. The $1.5\times$ threshold prevents unnecessary switching when the benefit is marginal.

\section{Scaling Mode Descriptions}

The three scaling modes control how the system manages the worker process lifecycle and request scheduling. Each mode is described below in terms of its programmatic function, benefits, drawbacks, utility, and significance during benchmarking and deployment.

\subsection{Horizontal Mode}

\textbf{Function.} Each inference request spawns a fresh Docker container running \texttt{pytorch\_container\_diffusion\_v3.py}. The autoscaler calls \texttt{worker\_pool.add\_worker()} which executes \texttt{docker run -d --gpus all -p <port>:50060 difs-diffusion-worker ...}. The new worker loads the model from scratch---deserializing safetensors weights from disk, constructing the pipeline, and running a 1-step warmup---then processes the request. DIFS\_OC\_v2 extends this with multi-worker support via \texttt{DIFS\_EXTRA\_WORKER\_PORTS} (comma-separated port list) and \texttt{run\_multiworker\_benchmark.py} that tests 1, 3, and 5 concurrent workers.

\textbf{Benefits.} Complete process isolation between requests. The multi-worker infrastructure enables distributing requests across multiple containers, providing genuine (though single-GPU-time-sliced) horizontal capacity. Docker encapsulation constrains resource usage.

\textbf{Drawbacks.} Highest cold-start latency of any mode. Every request pays the full cost of Docker creation, weight deserialization, and pipeline warmup. The INFaaS cost model's trigger conditions (Strategy A vs.\ B comparison) can delay container creation compared to simpler threshold-based approaches.

\textbf{Utility.} Best suited for workloads where isolation is paramount or bursty workloads where the delayed scale-down prevents premature container removal.

\textbf{Benchmark significance.} Horizontal establishes the cold-start load-time baseline. In this system, load times are compressed compared to the sibling project due to the INFaaS worker's efficient pipeline construction.

\textbf{Deployment significance.} When the autoscaler is active, \texttt{scale\_infaas()} handles horizontal decisions alongside vertical model-switching in one pass, using collected per-model QPS data and \texttt{model\_worker\_counts} replica tracking. Scale-down is delayed by \texttt{SCALE\_DOWN\_DELAY} (20 consecutive idle cycles) to prevent thrashing.

\subsection{Vertical Mode}

\textbf{Function.} A single persistent Docker worker is reused for all requests. When switching models, the autoscaler calls \texttt{worker\_pool.switch\_model()} which sends a \texttt{LoadModel} gRPC. The worker's \texttt{hot\_switch\_model()} checks an in-memory cache (\texttt{self.model\_cache}). On a cache hit, it repoints \texttt{self.current\_pipe}---milliseconds. On a miss, it calls \texttt{self.load\_model()} which detects the pipeline class, deserializes weights, runs warmup, caches the pipeline, and swaps the pointer. The worker exposes \texttt{WorkerStatus()} gRPC reporting \texttt{last\_switch\_time\_ms} for real-time cost tracking by the INFaaS autoscaler.

\textbf{Benefits.} Lowest load times. The in-memory cache makes repeated switches to the same model near-instantaneous. \texttt{WorkerStatus()} telemetry enables INFaaS cost decisions based on actual measured load costs rather than static estimates.

\textbf{Drawbacks.} Memory state accumulates across switches. No process isolation. The INFaaS cost model's automated switching adds orchestration overhead absent in simpler approaches.

\textbf{Utility.} Ideal for latency-sensitive multi-model serving. The per-model QPS tracking helps the INFaaS autoscaler optimize which variant to serve under changing workload popularity.

\textbf{Benchmark significance.} Vertical establishes the best-case baseline. The gap between vertical and horizontal times represents the maximum benefit of process reuse.

\textbf{Deployment significance.} The INFaaS cost model's \texttt{scale\_infaas()} handles vertical switching in the same pass as horizontal decisions. It compares Strategy A (current model mix) vs.\ B (fastest variant only) and triggers \texttt{\_trigger\_vertical\_switch()} when the cost benefit exceeds 1.5$\times$, selecting the least-busy worker via a score of \texttt{active\_requests $\times$ 1000 + average\_latency\_ms}.

\subsection{Hybrid Mode}

\textbf{Function.} The single unified method \texttt{scale\_infaas()} handles horizontal decisions, vertical model-switching, and batch-size tuning in one pass. The scheduler uses priority class queues (\textit{interactive}, \textit{balanced}, \textit{batch}) rather than the single FIFO used in pure vertical mode. Model switching follows the same hot-swap mechanism as vertical mode.

\textbf{Benefits.} Combines vertical hot-swapping with horizontal capacity expansion in a single coordinated pass, preventing the two-phase conflicts possible in sequential approaches. The INFaaS cost model ensures switching decisions are economically rational.

\textbf{Drawbacks.} Added complexity from evaluating all three axes every cycle. The cost comparison adds latency to the autoscaler loop.

\textbf{Utility.} Best general-purpose choice for production deployments with unpredictable workload patterns. The unified pass handles both bursty load and frequent model switching without manual mode selection.

\textbf{Benchmark significance.} Hybrid results are nearly indistinguishable from Vertical in this benchmark. The unified cost model's bookkeeping does not add measurable overhead at single-request granularity.

\textbf{Deployment significance.} \texttt{scale\_infaas()} evaluates all three decision axes in a single pass, eliminating sequential two-phase issues. Per-model QPS tracking and empirically measured load costs ground both horizontal and vertical decisions in actual runtime telemetry. Delayed scale-down prevents premature container removal during brief load dips.

% ============================================================
\chapter{Key Components}
% ============================================================

\section{server.py}
The main entry point. Initializes the gRPC server on port 50050, instantiates the \texttt{Scheduler}, \texttt{WorkerPool}, \texttt{AutoScaler}, and \texttt{Metrics} objects, and registers the \texttt{SchedulerService} gRPC servicer. Handles graceful shutdown on SIGINT/SIGTERM.

\section{scheduler.py}
The request scheduler. Maintains three priority queues (\textit{interactive}, \textit{balanced}, \textit{batch}) using the \texttt{WorkloadClassifier}. Tracks arrival/service counts and SLA violations.

\textbf{Scheduling policy.} The scheduler is neither pure FIFO nor pure EDF; the policy is scaling-mode dependent. In \texttt{vertical} mode requests are served strictly first-come, first-served (FIFO) from the single \texttt{request\_queue}. In \texttt{horizontal} and \texttt{hybrid} modes requests are classified into three strict-priority classes (\textit{interactive} $>$ \textit{balanced} $>$ \textit{batch}) and, within each class, dispatched earliest-deadline-first (EDF) by SLO deadline ($\texttt{deadline} = \texttt{arrival} + \texttt{latency\_slo}/1000$). Worker selection is a separate least-loaded policy: \texttt{get\_next\_worker()} scores each candidate as $\texttt{active\_requests} \times 1000 + \texttt{avg\_latency\_ms} + \texttt{gpu\_memory\_mb} \times 0.05$, plus $1000$ if the worker is busy and $5000$ on workload-class mismatch, and selects the minimum. In DIFS\_OC\_v2 the policy is identical; the INFaaS extension adds per-model replica tracking (\texttt{get\_replica\_count()}) and a unified \texttt{scale\_infaas()} autoscaler pass, but does not alter the scheduler's queueing or dispatch policy.


\section{worker\_pool.py}
Manages the lifecycle of worker processes. Spawns workers on dynamically assigned ports starting from 50060.

\section{autoscaler.py}
Dynamic scaling component. Monitors load metrics and decides when to scale workers up or down.

\section{pytorch\_container\_diffusion\_v3.py}
The GPU inference worker. Loads a Stable Diffusion pipeline onto the GPU (or via CPU offload), receives prompts over gRPC, runs the diffusion forward pass, and returns the generated image.

\section{model\_registry.py}
Maps integer model IDs to human-readable names and filenames.

