\subsection{Note on Load/Switch Measurement Equivalence}

The model-load-time plot (Figure~\ref{fig:load_time_bar}) and the switching-time plot (Figure~\ref{fig:switch_bar}) are numerically identical because both are generated from the same raw measurement---the \texttt{load\_time} field recorded during each inference request. In this single-worker benchmark, every request triggers a full model load from disk into GPU memory; there is no pre-warmed worker pool from which a pre-loaded model can be served. Consequently, the time to ``load'' a model for its first use equals the time to ``switch'' to it on any subsequent request. The two plots are presented separately to frame the same cost under different conceptual lenses: load time as a per-model infrastructure cost, and switching time as the per-request overhead the autoscaler encounters when transitioning between models during a workload.

