\noindent\textbf{Key observations:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Switching \emph{into} SD 3.5 Medium costs $\sim$6.0\,s on average (5.9--6.1\,s across all modes).
  \item Switching \emph{into} SD 2.1 costs $\sim$2.8\,s on average (1.8--4.6\,s across modes).
  \item SD 2.1 is 2.2$\times$ faster to load than SD 3.5 Medium due to its smaller weight file.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
\chapter{Analytic Discussion}
% ============================================================

\section{Architecture Comparison}

SD 2.1 uses a UNet-based architecture with 860M parameters, while SD 3.5 Medium uses a MM-DiT (Multimodal Diffusion Transformer) architecture with three text encoders (CLIP-L, CLIP-G, T5-XXL). This architectural difference is the primary driver of the performance gap.

\subsection{Latency Gap}
SD 3.5 Medium averages 23.92\,s latency vs SD 2.1's 14.79\,s---a 1.6$\times$ slowdown. SD 3.5 Medium's MM-DiT transformer, while more architecturally advanced, incurs higher computational cost per step than SD 2.1's UNet. The gap is driven by:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Three text encoders (CLIP-L, CLIP-G, T5-XXL) adding sequential CPU-offload overhead
  \item Larger transformer weights in the MM-DiT block requiring more GPU memory transfers
  \item Higher VAE decoder channel count extending the decode phase
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Memory Trade-off}
SD 3.5 Medium consumes 1072\,MB GPU peak memory vs SD 2.1's 142\,MB. The 7.5$\times$ memory increase is due to:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Three text encoders (CLIP-L: 246\,MB, CLIP-G: 1.4\,GB, T5-XXL: 9.8\,GB)
  \item Larger transformer weights in the MM-DiT block
  \item VAE decoder with higher channel count
\end{itemize}

\section{Scaling Mode Analysis}

\subsection{SD 2.1 Scaling Mode Behavior}
SD 2.1 shows negligible latency difference across all three modes: horizontal (15.58\,s), vertical (12.87\,s), and hybrid (15.91\,s). The vertical mode is fastest by $\sim$2.7\,s, which may reflect slight CPU-offload scheduling variation rather than a structural advantage.

\subsection{SD 3.5 Medium Scaling Mode Behavior}
SD 3.5 Medium shows consistent latency across all three modes: horizontal 25.64\,s, vertical 22.86\,s, hybrid 23.27\,s. The variation of 2.78\,s (12.1\%) between the fastest and slowest mode is primarily due to measurement noise and CPU-offload scheduling variation; inference time itself remains consistent.

\subsection{Load Time Differences}
SD 2.1 loads in 2.75\,s (averaged across modes), reflecting its small weight footprint. SD 3.5 Medium loads in 6.04\,s---2.2$\times$ longer than SD 2.1---consistent with its larger MM-DiT transformer and three text encoders. The load time for both models is tightly clustered across modes, with no extreme cold-start outlier.

\section{Why Scaling Mode Doesn't Affect Single-Worker Latency}

The scaling mode controls \emph{worker management}---how workers are created, destroyed, and routed. Once a model is loaded into VRAM and inference begins, the forward pass executes identically regardless of mode. The latency is dominated by the model's forward pass through the transformer/UNet and VAE, which takes $\sim$23--26\,s for SD 3.5 Medium and $\sim$13--16\,s for SD 2.1. The only differences between scaling modes appear in the model load/switch time (2.75\,s for SD 2.1, 6.04\,s for SD 3.5 Medium), which occurs \emph{before} the timed inference window.

\textbf{Why the two claims are consistent: a phase decomposition.}
A request's wall-clock duration is the sum of two separable phases, $T_{\mathrm{job}} = T_{\mathrm{load/switch}} + T_{\mathrm{inference}}$. $T_{\mathrm{inference}}$ is the GPU-bound forward pass (denoising UNet/transformer plus VAE decoding) measured inside the timed window; $T_{\mathrm{load/switch}}$ is the worker-provisioning cost paid \emph{before} that window opens---deserializing weights from disk and constructing the diffusion pipeline. The scaling mode controls only the second term: Horizontal pays a full cold start, Vertical pays an in-memory hot-swap of an already-cached pipeline, and Hybrid sequences both. Once a pipeline is resident in VRAM, the forward pass is the same arithmetic regardless of mode, so $T_{\mathrm{inference}}$ is statistically identical across modes---this is the ``orthogonal to single-worker latency'' result. But $T_{\mathrm{load/switch}}$ is genuinely mode-dependent, and $T_{\mathrm{job}}$ inherits that dependence: for SD~2.1, the 2.75\,s load against $\sim$13--16\,s of inference is roughly 15--17\% of total job time; for SD~3.5~Medium, the 6.04\,s load against $\sim$23--26\,s of inference is roughly 19--21\%. The two claims are therefore not in tension: one characterises the compute stage (mode-independent), the other the provisioning stage that precedes it (mode-dependent).

\section{Scaling Mode Naming Convention and Architecture Comparison}

\subsection{Naming Convention}

Unlike the sibling project \textit{DIFS\_OC\_v1}, which uses the \textbf{P-} prefix (P-Horizontal, P-Vertical, P-Hybrid) to denote \textbf{pseudo-scaling} on a single GPU, this report drops the prefix entirely. The scaling modes are labelled simply as Horizontal, Vertical, and Hybrid. The ``P-'' prefix in \textit{DIFS\_OC\_v1} explicitly distinguishes single-GPU operation from true distributed infrastructure; \textit{DIFS\_OC\_v2} omits it because the scaling modes are treated as the implementation itself rather than a simulation of multi-node behaviour.

Although both projects run on the same single-GPU hardware, \textit{DIFS\_OC\_v2} implements a more sophisticated INFaaS-style cost-model autoscaler in Python, treats model hot-switching as an automated vertical-scaling primitive, and tracks per-model replica counts with delayed scale-down---justifying the production-style naming.

\subsection{Architecture Comparison}

Table~\ref{tab:scaling_arch} summarises the key architectural and implementational distinctions between the two projects for each scaling mode.

