\subsection{Equivalence Boundaries}

Despite the architectural differences in Table~\ref{tab:scaling_arch}, the scaling mode mechanics---worker lifecycle (Docker spawn vs.\ process reuse), queuing strategy (priority class queues vs.\ single FIFO), and hot-swap mechanism---are identical for equivalent modes across the two projects. The Horizontal worker behaviour in DIFS\_OC\_v2 is programmatically the same as P-Horizontal in DIFS\_OC\_v1; the same holds for Vertical and Hybrid. Nevertheless, the modes produce different benchmark numbers and would behave differently in deployment for three reasons:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]

\item \textbf{Autoscaler logic (deployment only).} During benchmarking, both projects set \texttt{DIFS\_NO\_AUTOSCALER=1} to isolate per-model measurements, so the autoscaler is inactive. In a live deployment, however, the autoscaler determines \emph{when} to add/remove workers or trigger a model switch. DIFS\_OC\_v1 uses a simple threshold-based policy ($\rho > 0.80$ scale up, $\rho < 0.30$ scale down, queue-depth batch tuning), while DIFS\_OC\_v2 uses an INFaaS-style cost model that compares the cost of the current model mix against the cost of using only the fastest-loading variant, with a configurable 1.5$\times$ threshold. Consequently, the same workload would trigger scale events at different times---or not at all---depending on which autoscaler is active. The mode mechanics ({\itshape what} happens when a scale event fires) remain the same, but the triggering logic ({\itshape whether} it fires) differs.

\item \textbf{Multi-worker infrastructure.} DIFS\_OC\_v2 supports registering multiple workers via the \texttt{DIFS\_EXTRA\_WORKER\_PORTS} environment variable and provides \texttt{start\_worker\_at\_port()}/\texttt{stop\_worker\_at\_port()} in the process manager. Its \texttt{run\_multiworker\_benchmark.py} tests scenarios with 1, 3, and 5 concurrent workers. DIFS\_OC\_v1 uses a single external worker with no dynamic port allocation. In a multi-worker deployment, the horizontal mode behaviour would therefore diverge: OC\_v2 can distribute requests across several containers, while OC\_v1 is limited to one.

\item \textbf{Environmental factors.} The benchmark results for the same mode differ between the two projects (e.g.\ SD-v1.4-dir load time: 1.63\,s in DIFS\_OC\_v2 Horizontal vs.\ 1.83\,s in DIFS\_OC\_v1 P-Horizontal). These differences arise from \texttt{diffusers} library version mismatches, differing system package states, GPU contention patterns at run time, and filesystem caching effects---not from mode implementation differences. The mode mechanics are identical, but the results are measured in environments that are similar, not identical.

\end{enumerate}

