% ============================================================
\chapter{Conclusion}
% ============================================================

This report presented a focused benchmark comparing SD 2.1 and SD 3.5 Medium across 3 scaling modes on an NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPU. SD 2.1 achieves 1.6$\times$ lower latency than SD 3.5 Medium (14.79\,s vs 23.92\,s) while consuming 7.5$\times$ less GPU memory (142\,MB vs 1072\,MB). SD 3.5 Medium's MM-DiT transformer trades substantially higher memory for slightly higher latency. Both models run successfully using sequential CPU offload, confirming the feasibility of serving diverse Stable Diffusion model generations on a single GPU.

\textbf{Future Work:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Benchmark with higher step counts (20, 50) to confirm the relative latency ratio holds.
  \item Measure image quality (FID, CLIP score) to determine if SD 3.5 Medium also produces better images.
  \item Test multi-GPU configurations to assess scaling potential.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Glossary
% ============================================================
\newpage
\appendix
\chapter{Glossary of Terms}

The following list defines all technical terms used in the tables and figures throughout this report.

\begin{description}[style=nextline]
  \item[\textbf{CPU Offloading}] Temporarily moving model weights to CPU RAM to fit larger models in GPU memory. Adds latency but enables models that exceed VRAM.
  \item[\textbf{Cold-Switch}] Stop worker container, start new one with a different model. Fully general but slower.
  \item[\textbf{FID}] Frechet Inception Distance. Measures image quality vs.\ real COCO images. Lower = better.
  \item[\textbf{GPU Memory (MB)}] Peak VRAM usage during inference (weights + activations).
  \item[\textbf{Hot-Switch}] In-memory model weight transfer on a running worker. Faster than restarting the container.
  \item[\textbf{Latency (s)}] Time from request submission to image delivery. Lower is better.
  \item[\textbf{MM-DiT}] Multi-Modal Diffusion Transformer. SD 3.5's architecture replacing the UNet with a transformer that jointly processes text and image latents.
  \item[\textbf{Model Load Time}] Time to deserialize weights from disk and allocate GPU memory.
  \item[\textbf{P50 / P95 / P99 Latency}] Median, 95th percentile, and 99th percentile request latencies.
  \item[\textbf{SD-2.1}] Stable Diffusion 2.1 (865M params). UNet-based architecture, 768$\times$768 max resolution.
  \item[\textbf{SD-3.5-Medium}] Stable Diffusion 3.5 Medium (2.5B params). MM-DiT transformer architecture with three text encoders (CLIP$\times$2 + T5-XXL).
  \item[\textbf{Sampler}] Diffusion noise scheduler (Euler-a = Ancestral Euler, the default).
  \item[\textbf{Scaling Mode}] \emph{Horizontal}: add/remove workers. \emph{Vertical}: switch model on a worker. \emph{Hybrid}: both.
  \item[\textbf{Steps}] Denoising iterations. More steps = better quality but linearly higher latency. Default: 10 (two-model report).
  \item[\textbf{T5-XXL Encoder}] Google's T5 text encoder (4.7B params) used by SD 3.5 for richer text understanding. Requires CPU offloading on single GPUs.
  \item[\textbf{Throughput (img/s)}] Images generated per second ($1 / \text{avg latency}$). Higher is better.
  \item[\textbf{Wall Time}] Total elapsed clock time for the entire benchmark run.
  \item[\textbf{Worker}] Docker container/subprocess running the diffusion pipeline on a GPU port.
  \item[\textbf{Workload}] Number of requests per benchmark run. Each generates one image.
\end{description}

